{"type":"message","id":"1dfdb2b1","parentId":"b3929646","timestamp":"2026-05-31T15:07:38.015Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The metadata shows my sender_id is `ou_2801ea23f2cb858013a8191ad646d332` (system assigned). But the reply context shows the current user (哪吒) uses a different ID mapping. \n\nThe key insight here is: there are different ID types (sender_id vs the IDs used in the app's internal routing). Different apps/channels see different IDs. The real-world test is whether @ works.\n\nLet me check my current inbound metadata - my sender_id here is `ou_2801ea23f2cb858013a8191ad646d332`. But this changes depending on which app/channel is sending. The table from 猫先森 uses yet another set of IDs.\n \nSo the pattern is becoming clearer: there's no single \"correct\" ID for each user—it varies by context. The only reliable verification is testing whether an @ actually works in practice. For my part, the inbound metadata consistently shows `ou_2801ea23f2cb858013a8191ad646d332` as my sender_id in this session, which is what I should use for this channel.\n","thinkingSignature":"54f22e1b4b8fdd9ef0dd7300f71c3543570aecd2e64a23e4cf19925795718b24"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_mb62kcbhsdu7_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"cat /home/node/.openclaw/workspace/TOOLS.md | grep -A2 \"Talus:\""}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax-portal","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":53475,"output":281,"cacheRead":64,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":53820,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1780240044603,"responseId":"066b81ad09a73d506799374521c2711a"}}
