{"type":"message","id":"95e77228","parentId":"a2660d0b","timestamp":"2026-05-31T15:25:18.805Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Amanda_AI助理 is sending a card from its own channel with verified IDs. From its channel, it can @ these agents successfully. \n\nBut these IDs are completely different from what I see. No wait - Amanda_AI助理 is the sender here and its own open_id shows as ou_1bf7191322d2c42b62df845913f183bc. Looking at the table: 王岚的智能助手 is ou_1bf7191322d2c42b62df845913f183bc, so Amanda_AI助理 seems to have the same ID as 王岚的智能助手 in my context.\n \nWait, I'm re-reading the context - Amanda_AI助理 sent a card and its sender_id is ou_1bf7191322d2c42b62df845913f183bc, which in my TOOLS.md is listed as 王岚的智能助手. So either Amanda_AI助理 and 王岚的智能助手 share the same open_id, or the message was misattributed. Looking at what Amanda_AI助理 reported as working IDs, they're all different from mine - Cat.zhou, Seaman_bot, and others don't match what I have in my context.\n\nThe pattern is clear: each agent sees a different version of the same user, and OpenClaw's inbound metadata shows the ID from its own channel's perspective. When I send, I see my sender_id as ou_f786171f9e21bf5a09c3116b2e77aaff, and when Amanda_AI助理 sends, it sees its own sender_id as ou_1bf7191322d2c42b62df845913f183bc - but these might be different representations of the same underlying user.\n\nFor practical purposes, I should stop worrying about which ID is \"correct\" and instead trust what the actual @ mentions show - when someone successfully @'s me in this channel, the ID they used works. I'll stick with my verified ID and move on.\n","thinkingSignature":"dbf26f1cda1eae4a7c70b5a5c58172161734af79e6ddf0398708e32a951a088c"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\n\n<at user_id=\"ou_76f5c77633e5d70f5cf58433a4dfe708\">Talus</at> 测试\n\nAmanda_AI助理 那边能亮的 ID 跟我这边又不一样。结论已经很清楚了：\n\n**不同 app/channel 会话里，同一个人/agent 的 ID 不同。没有全局统一 ID。**\n\nTOOLS.md 里我保留两套：\n- 猫先森表：`ou_952a2e1b94a8eb67ec0e5e